# BLS Signature Verification — "I know a signature on m under pk"
#
# Proves knowledge of a BLS signature `sig` (kept secret) such that
#   e(sig, g2) == e(hm, pk)
# where `hm` is the hash-to-curve of message m (computed off-chain
# and provided as a public G1 input), and `pk` is the signer's public
# key in G2.
#
# Standard BLS:
#   sig = [sk] H_to_G1(m)        in G1
#   pk  = [sk] g2                in G2
# Verifier checks bilinearity: e(sig, g2) == e(H_to_G1(m), pk)
#
# This is a useful primitive on its own — proving you hold a valid
# signature on a known message without revealing the signature itself
# (e.g. for anonymous credential systems or signature aggregation).
#
# Note: hash-to-curve is too expensive in-circuit, so `hm` is computed
# off-chain and provided as a public input. The circuit just verifies
# the pairing equality.
#
# This demonstrates: Secret G1 input, Public G1 input, Public G2 input,
# pairing equality check (Fq12 == Fq12).

Secret G1 Inputs: (sig)
Public G1 Inputs: (hm)
Public G2 Inputs: (pk)
Constants:
    g2  # G2 generator

compute: lhs = e(sig, g2)
compute: rhs = e(hm, pk)
check: lhs == rhs
